 # Depth and Geometry Supervision
 
-Also called: Geometric Regularization, Depth-Aware Training, Geometry Priors
+Also called: Geometric Regularization, Depth Priors, Geometric Constraints
 
 ## Overview
-Depth and Geometry Supervision methods augment the standard photometric loss in Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) training with explicit geometric constraints derived from depth estimates or structural priors [S1, S5]. These methods solve the shape-radiance ambiguity and overfitting issues that arise in sparse-view or textureless scenarios by enforcing that the rendered density distribution aligns with known geometric structures [S1, S8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on semantic consistency [11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md] or diffusion priors [7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md], this family explicitly minimizes the discrepancy between rendered depth/density and external depth maps, sparse point clouds, or internal geometric consistency metrics [S1, S5].
+Depth and Geometry Supervision methods accelerate Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization and improve geometric accuracy in sparse-view or ill-posed settings by incorporating external or internal geometric priors into the training loop [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md]. Unlike pure photometric approaches that rely solely on color reconstruction, these methods constrain the density field $\sigma$ and rendering weights $w_i$ to align with estimated surface locations, thereby mitigating shape-radiance ambiguity and preventing degenerate solutions such as "floaters" or image-plane collapse [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
