 ## Background
-The concept of using depth priors to regularize NeRF optimization originated with the recognition that photometric loss alone is insufficient for sparse-view reconstruction, leading to "floaters" and shape-radiance ambiguity [S1, S5]. Early work like DS-NeRF introduced sparse 3D point supervision to guide the density field [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md]. Subsequent methods expanded this to dense monocular depth priors [S1, 9371-depth-structural-consistency-regularization.md], stereo depth with statistical refinement [S1, 10185-statistical-depth-refinement.md], and architectural priors for indoor scenes [S4]. The family has evolved to include indirect supervision via KL divergence [S5, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md] and consistency losses between coarse-fine sampling stages [S1, Coarse-Fine Consistency Loss]. It is widely used in applications requiring high geometric fidelity from limited views, such as autonomous navigation, architectural modeling, and novel view synthesis for VR [S1, S2, S4, S6].
+Depth and geometry supervision in NeRFs originated from the observation that photometric loss alone is insufficient for recovering accurate geometry, especially in sparse-view or textureless scenarios [S1, 8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md]. Early work by [DS-NeRF](8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md) demonstrated that leveraging sparse 3D keypoints from Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines could significantly accelerate convergence and improve geometric fidelity [S1, S2][S3]. Subsequent research expanded to include dense depth priors from monocular estimators [S1, 9371-depth-structural-consistency-regularization.md], stereo matching [S1, 10185-statistical-depth-refinement.md], and self-supervised geometric constraints [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md]. These methods are widely used in applications requiring high-fidelity 3D reconstruction from limited views, such as robotics, augmented reality, and cultural heritage digitization [S1, S4, S8].
 
